{"name":"Macaw Green","hex":"#9cad3b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"macaw","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various parrots of the genera Ara, Anodorhynchus, Cyanopsitta, Orthopsittaca, Primolius and Diopsittaca of Central and South America, including the largest parrots and characterized by long sabre-shaped tails, curved powerful bills, and usually brilliant plumage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/macaw"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Macaw","blocks":[{"text":"Macaws are a group of large New World parrots that are long-tailed and often colorful, in the tribe Arini. They are popular in aviculture or as companion parrots, although there are conservation concerns about several species in the wild."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Ara_macao%2C_Ara_ararauna_and_Ara_militaris.jpg/330px-Ara_macao%2C_Ara_ararauna_and_Ara_militaris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macaw","wikidata":"Q219035"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Glaucous_Macaw.jpg/330px-Glaucous_Macaw.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Glaucous_Macaw.jpg/330px-Glaucous_Macaw.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Glaucous macaw (behind hyacinth macaw) and other macaws","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGlaucous_Macaw.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Ara_ararauna_and_Ara_glaucogularis_-captive_in_Canada-8a.jpg/500px-Ara_ararauna_and_Ara_glaucogularis_-captive_in_Canada-8a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Ara_ararauna_and_Ara_glaucogularis_-captive_in_Canada-8a.jpg/500px-Ara_ararauna_and_Ara_glaucogularis_-captive_in_Canada-8a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Blue-and-yellow macaw (left) and blue-throated macaw (right)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAra_ararauna_and_Ara_glaucogularis_-captive_in_Canada-8a.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Anonimo_portoghese%2C_carta_na